ghts on how the world is changing, published every few days.</p></header><a href="/latest?type=data-insight" class="owid-btn owid-btn--outline-vermillion latest-data-insights-block__see-all-data-insights-button body-3-medium span-cols-4 col-start-10 span-sm-cols-12 col-sm-start-2"><span>See all Data Insights</span><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right owid-btn--icon-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></a><div class="latest-data-insights span-cols-12 col-start-2"><ul class="latest-data-insights__card-container"><li class="latest-data-insights__card latest-data-insights__card--snapped"><a class="latest-data-insights__card__data-insight" href="/data-insights/money-spent-on-building-data-centers-in-the-us-has-grown-5-fold-since-late-2022" aria-labelledby="latest-data-insights__card-title-0"><div class="image latest-data-insights__card-left"><picture><source srcSet="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/fac09369-5a17-4aa6-58e7-e74992338e00/w=48 48w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/fac09369-5a17-4aa6-58e7-e74992338e00/w=100 100w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/fac09369-5a17-4aa6-58e7-e74992338e00/w=350 350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/fac09369-5a17-4aa6-58e7-e74992338e00/w=850 850w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/fac09369-5a17-4aa6-58e7-e74992338e00/w=1350 1350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/fac09369-5a17-4aa6-58e7-e74992338e00/w=1620 1620w" sizes="(max-width: 960px) 95vw, (min-width: 960px) 533px"/><img src="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/fac09369-5a17-4aa6-58e7-e74992338e00/w=1620" alt="Line chart of monthly on-site construction spending to build data centers in the United States where spending is relatively flat from 2015 to 2021 and then rises sharply after late 2022 to roughly five-fold higher levels; the series excludes IT hardware like servers and storage. Source: U.S. Census Bureau (2026); U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (2026). License: CC BY." loading="lazy" width="1620" height="1620"/></picture></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-right"><p class="data-insight-dateline latest-data-insights__card-dateline"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="calendar-day" class="svg-inline--fa fa-calendar-day data-insight-dateline__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M128 0c17.7 0 32 14.3 32 32l0 32 128 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32s32 14.3 32 32l0 32 32 0c35.3 0 64 28.7 64 64l0 288c0 35.3-28.7 64-64 64L64 480c-35.3 0-64-28.7-64-64L0 128C0 92.7 28.7 64 64 64l32 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32zm0 256c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32l0 64c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32l64 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32l0-64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32l-64 0z"></path></svg>Yesterday</p><h3 id="latest-data-insights__card-title-0" class="latest-data-insights__card-title">Money spent on building data centers in the US has grown 5-fold since late 2022</h3><p class="latest-data-insights__card-authors">Edouard Mathieu</p><div class="latest-data-insights__card-body"><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">The United States is home to </span><span><span class="">thousands of data centers</span></span><span class="">. They are part of the infrastructure that powers many digital services, including email, gaming, streaming, social media, and, increasingly, artificial intelligence (AI).</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">As this chart shows, between 2018 and 2022, spending on building data centers in the US hovered between $500 million and $1 billion per month (when adjusted for inflation).</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">But since late 2022, large investments in AI have led to a rapid growth in data center construction — both by expanding existing facilities and building new, larger ones.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">The latest figures released by the US Census Bureau estimate that $4.4 billion was spent in June 2026 alone — a 5-fold increase compared to November 2022, when ChatGPT was released by OpenAI.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">These figures cover privately-funded construction and represent only the money spent on building the actual facilities, including materials and construction labor. They don’t include the cost of IT hardware like servers and storage, which can represent a very large share of total investment.</span></p><div class="article-block__cta cta">Explore our interactive charts on artificial intelligence<svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-continue"><span class="body-3-medium-underlined">Continue reading</span> <svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:10px"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></a></li><li class="latest-data-insights__card"><a class="latest-data-insights__card__data-insight" href="/data-insights/how-often-are-people-infected-with-malaria" aria-labelledby="latest-data-insights__card-title-1"><div class="image latest-data-insights__card-left"><picture><source srcSet="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/6ec31df4-09e8-41f4-bedc-847c3bdeb400/w=48 48w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/6ec31df4-09e8-41f4-bedc-847c3bdeb400/w=100 100w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/6ec31df4-09e8-41f4-bedc-847c3bdeb400/w=350 350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/6ec31df4-09e8-41f4-bedc-847c3bdeb400/w=850 850w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/6ec31df4-09e8-41f4-bedc-847c3bdeb400/w=1350 1350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/6ec31df4-09e8-41f4-bedc-847c3bdeb400/w=1620 1620w" sizes="(max-width: 960px) 95vw, (min-width: 960px) 533px"/><img src="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/6ec31df4-09e8-41f4-bedc-847c3bdeb400/w=1620" alt="Horizontal arrow chart of new malaria cases per 100 people in selected high-burden countries where it compares values in 2000 and 2023 to show declines over time. The chart shows cases falling from about 41 to 49 in 2000 down to roughly 28 to 36 in 2023, with most worst-affected countries still around 30 cases per 100 people. Source: IHME, Global Burden of Disease (2025). License: CC BY." loading="lazy" width="1620" height="1620"/></picture></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-right"><p class="data-insight-dateline latest-data-insights__card-dateline"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="calendar-day" class="svg-inline--fa fa-calendar-day data-insight-dateline__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M128 0c17.7 0 32 14.3 32 32l0 32 128 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32s32 14.3 32 32l0 32 32 0c35.3 0 64 28.7 64 64l0 288c0 35.3-28.7 64-64 64L64 480c-35.3 0-64-28.7-64-64L0 128C0 92.7 28.7 64 64 64l32 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32zm0 256c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32l0 64c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32l64 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32l0-64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32l-64 0z"></path></svg>August 29</p><h3 id="latest-data-insights__card-title-1" class="latest-data-insights__card-title">How often are people infected with malaria?</h3><p class="latest-data-insights__card-authors">Hannah Ritchie</p><div class="latest-data-insights__card-body"><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">Malaria is an infectious disease that kills over </span><span><span class="">600,000 people</span></span><span class=""> a year. Most of them, children.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">Its high death toll is at least partly explained by the fact that there are so many infections every year: an estimated </span><span><span class="">260 to 310 million</span></span><span class="">.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">I was curious to know how often people living in the worst-affected areas are infected with malaria. In the chart, you can see estimates of new cases per 100 people across high-burden countries. In recent decades, infection rates have declined but remain very high. There are around 30 new cases per 100 people each year.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">It would be tempting to simplify this number and say that 30% of people living in Nigeria are infected each year. But that’s probably far from reality.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">This is because the same people are often reinfected. When I looked at the scientific literature, I was surprised by how often. A </span><span><span class="">study</span></span><span class=""> focused on children in Uganda found that those without preventative measures were infected around five times per year. Another found that in Burkina Faso, it was </span><span><span class="">up to six times</span></span><span class="">.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">So, the reality behind the “30 cases per 100 people” average is probably very skewed. Many — particularly children who have not built up immunity — are infected multiple times per year, while most of the population is not infected at all.</span></p><div class="article-block__cta cta">Explore more data on the global burden of malaria<svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-continue"><span class="body-3-medium-underlined">Continue reading</span> <svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:10px"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></a></li><li class="latest-data-insights__card"><a class="latest-data-insights__card__data-insight" href="/data-insights/drowning-is-a-common-cause-of-death-among-children-the-world-has-made-progress-but-more-can-be-done" aria-labelledby="latest-data-insights__card-title-2"><div class="image latest-data-insights__card-left"><picture><source srcSet="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/10126e3d-64fb-4481-d289-c005f7f99000/w=48 48w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/10126e3d-64fb-4481-d289-c005f7f99000/w=100 100w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/10126e3d-64fb-4481-d289-c005f7f99000/w=350 350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/10126e3d-64fb-4481-d289-c005f7f99000/w=850 850w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/10126e3d-64fb-4481-d289-c005f7f99000/w=1350 1350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/10126e3d-64fb-4481-d289-c005f7f99000/w=1620 1620w" sizes="(max-width: 960px) 95vw, (min-width: 960px) 533px"/><img src="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/10126e3d-64fb-4481-d289-c005f7f99000/w=1620" alt="Line chart of estimated global drowning death rates per 100,000 children for ages under 5 and ages 5 to 14 from 1980 to 2023, where both lines decline steadily and the under-5 rate remains consistently higher than the 5 to 14 rate. Source: IHME, Global Burden of Disease (2025). License: CC BY. Note: estimates include unintentional drownings (ponds, wells, bathtubs) and exclude drownings in floods, boat accidents, suicides, and homicides." loading="lazy" width="1620" height="1620"/></picture></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-right"><p class="data-insight-dateline latest-data-insights__card-dateline"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="calendar-day" class="svg-inline--fa fa-calendar-day data-insight-dateline__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M128 0c17.7 0 32 14.3 32 32l0 32 128 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32s32 14.3 32 32l0 32 32 0c35.3 0 64 28.7 64 64l0 288c0 35.3-28.7 64-64 64L64 480c-35.3 0-64-28.7-64-64L0 128C0 92.7 28.7 64 64 64l32 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32zm0 256c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32l0 64c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32l64 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32l0-64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32l-64 0z"></path></svg>August 27</p><h3 id="latest-data-insights__card-title-2" class="latest-data-insights__card-title">Drowning is a common cause of death among children — the world has made progress, but more can be done</h3><p class="latest-data-insights__card-authors">Esteban Ortiz-Ospina</p><div class="latest-data-insights__card-body"><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">More than 100,000 children under the age of 15 died from drowning in 2023. I found this statistic staggering when I first saw it. Drowning is one of the </span><span><span class="">top causes of death</span></span><span class=""> among older children.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">The World Health Organization </span><span><span class="">explains</span></span><span class=""> that around 90% of all drowning deaths occur in low- and middle-income countries. There, much of the risk comes from everyday exposure to water in ponds, rivers, and lakes used for bathing and washing clothes, or open wells </span><span><span class="">used to collect water</span></span><span class="">.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">Deaths like these can be prevented, and they increasingly are. Drowning used to be far more common than it is today. That’s the focus of the chart.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">In 1980, almost 30 children under five died from drowning for every 100,000 children in that age group. By 2023, this rate had fallen to 7.4. Death rates among older children also fell substantially.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">Targeted measures can reduce drowning risks. These include covering wells, using doorway barriers or playpens to keep young children away from water, and water-safety training, </span><span><span class="">among others</span></span><span class="">.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">Rising living standards matter as well, and much of the decline in the chart reflects this. As many countries have become richer, </span><span><span class="">access to safe water has improved</span></span><span class="">, so fewer families depend on ponds and open wells for everyday needs.</span></p><div class="article-block__cta cta">Drowning is one of many causes of child deaths. Which ones are the most common? We recently built a visualization that lets you explore this for any country, across different age groups.<svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-continue"><span class="body-3-medium-underlined">Continue reading</span> <svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:10px"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></a></li><li class="latest-data-insights__card"><a class="latest-data-insights__card__data-insight" href="/data-insights/meat-preferences-vary-a-lot-across-different-countries" aria-labelledby="latest-data-insights__card-title-3"><div class="image latest-data-insights__card-left"><picture><source srcSet="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/af3f7962-1cb1-4558-7034-7cc6504a5100/w=48 48w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/af3f7962-1cb1-4558-7034-7cc6504a5100/w=100 100w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/af3f7962-1cb1-4558-7034-7cc6504a5100/w=350 350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/af3f7962-1cb1-4558-7034-7cc6504a5100/w=850 850w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/af3f7962-1cb1-4558-7034-7cc6504a5100/w=1350 1350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/af3f7962-1cb1-4558-7034-7cc6504a5100/w=1620 1620w" sizes="(max-width: 960px) 95vw, (min-width: 960px) 533px"/><img src="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/af3f7962-1cb1-4558-7034-7cc6504a5100/w=1620" alt="Stacked bar chart of meat supply breakdown by type across countries where the visualization shows wide variation in dominant meats — for example, Brazil and Argentina have large poultry and beef shares, Germany and China have large pork shares, and India and Japan have high fish and seafood shares. Data are for 2023 except Japan (2022); source: Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (2025). License: CC BY." loading="lazy" width="1620" height="1620"/></picture></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-right"><p class="data-insight-dateline latest-data-insights__card-dateline"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="calendar-day" class="svg-inline--fa fa-calendar-day data-insight-dateline__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M128 0c17.7 0 32 14.3 32 32l0 32 128 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32s32 14.3 32 32l0 32 32 0c35.3 0 64 28.7 64 64l0 288c0 35.3-28.7 64-64 64L64 480c-35.3 0-64-28.7-64-64L0 128C0 92.7 28.7 64 64 64l32 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32zm0 256c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32l0 64c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32l64 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32l0-64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32l-64 0z"></path></svg>August 25</p><h3 id="latest-data-insights__card-title-3" class="latest-data-insights__card-title">Meat preferences vary a lot across different countries</h3><p class="latest-data-insights__card-authors">Hannah Ritchie and Pablo Arriagada</p><div class="latest-data-insights__card-body"><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">America’s most popular type of meat is chicken. In Argentina, it’s beef. And in Japan, it’s seafood.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">There are large differences in the popularity of meat types across the world.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">In the chart, you can see the share of supply that comes from different types of meat: poultry, beef, pork, sheep and goat, and fish and seafood. I’ve picked just a selection of countries that highlight some of the variation across the world.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">Of course, countries also eat very different </span><span><span class="">amounts</span></span><span class=""> of meat; this chart focuses on the relative quantities in national diets.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">This data comes from the </span><span><span class="">Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations</span></span><span class="">.</span></p><div class="article-block__cta cta">Explore the most popular types of meat in your country in the global dataset<svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-continue"><span class="body-3-medium-underlined">Continue reading</span> <svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:10px"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></a></li><li class="latest-data-insights__card"><a class="latest-data-insights__card__data-insight" href="/data-insights/thailands-population-may-have-already-peaked" aria-labelledby="latest-data-insights__card-title-4"><div class="image latest-data-insights__card-left"><picture><source srcSet="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/d2b43c12-1228-4e37-35ae-ebf352782f00/w=48 48w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/d2b43c12-1228-4e37-35ae-ebf352782f00/w=100 100w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/d2b43c12-1228-4e37-35ae-ebf352782f00/w=350 350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/d2b43c12-1228-4e37-35ae-ebf352782f00/w=850 850w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/d2b43c12-1228-4e37-35ae-ebf352782f00/w=1350 1350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/d2b43c12-1228-4e37-35ae-ebf352782f00/w=1620 1620w" sizes="(max-width: 960px) 95vw, (min-width: 960px) 533px"/><img src="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/d2b43c12-1228-4e37-35ae-ebf352782f00/w=1620" alt="Line chart of Thailand&#x27;s population from 1950 to 2100 where UN estimates show a rise to a peak around 2020 to 2025 at about 70 million, followed by a projected decline through 2100. Data source: UN, World Population Prospects (2024). License: CC BY." loading="lazy" width="1620" height="1620"/></picture></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-right"><p class="data-insight-dateline latest-data-insights__card-dateline"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="calendar-day" class="svg-inline--fa fa-calendar-day data-insight-dateline__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M128 0c17.7 0 32 14.3 32 32l0 32 128 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32s32 14.3 32 32l0 32 32 0c35.3 0 64 28.7 64 64l0 288c0 35.3-28.7 64-64 64L64 480c-35.3 0-64-28.7-64-64L0 128C0 92.7 28.7 64 64 64l32 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32zm0 256c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32l0 64c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32l64 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32l0-64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32l-64 0z"></path></svg>August 22</p><h3 id="latest-data-insights__card-title-4" class="latest-data-insights__card-title">Thailand’s population may have already peaked</h3><p class="latest-data-insights__card-authors">Hannah Ritchie, Edouard Mathieu, and Lucas Rodés-Guirao</p><div class="latest-data-insights__card-body"><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">Many countries are in a fairly unique demographic position in history: their populations are shrinking not due to a temporary shock but to sustained structural decline.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">If we look at the United Nations’ population estimates and medium projections, 66 countries and territories </span><span><span class="">had already passed</span></span><span class=""> peak population this year. Almost all of them are in Europe and East Asia.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">But there is one country in Southeast Asia in this position: Thailand. As you can see in the chart, the UN projects that its population will continue to fall throughout this century.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">This is the result of a rapid drop in fertility rates. In 1970, women in Thailand had an average of 6 children; twenty years later, that had fallen to 2. This was as fast as </span><span><span class="">China’s decline</span></span><span class="">. Today, the rate is at most 1.2 children per woman, and some national statistics put it closer to 1.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">What makes Thailand unusual is that this happened while it was still a </span><span><span class="">relatively poor country</span></span><span class="">. In 1970, it launched its National Family Planning Program. This was voluntary and non-coercive, but effectively increased contraceptive use, family planning access, and education levels for women.</span></p><div class="article-block__cta cta">Explore which other countries may have already peaked<svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-continue"><span class="body-3-medium-underlined">Continue reading</span> <svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:10px"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></a></li><li class="latest-data-insights__card"><a class="latest-data-insights__card__data-insight" href="/data-insights/four-developed-countries-met-the-uns-target-for-foreign-aid-in2025" aria-labelledby="latest-data-insights__card-title-5"><div class="image latest-data-insights__card-left"><picture><source srcSet="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/1d41ad85-1bae-49ff-b3bd-d2a8da39df00/w=48 48w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/1d41ad85-1bae-49ff-b3bd-d2a8da39df00/w=100 100w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/1d41ad85-1bae-49ff-b3bd-d2a8da39df00/w=350 350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/1d41ad85-1bae-49ff-b3bd-d2a8da39df00/w=850 850w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/1d41ad85-1bae-49ff-b3bd-d2a8da39df00/w=1350 1350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/1d41ad85-1bae-49ff-b3bd-d2a8da39df00/w=1620 1620w" sizes="(max-width: 960px) 95vw, (min-width: 960px) 533px"/><img src="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/1d41ad85-1bae-49ff-b3bd-d2a8da39df00/w=1620" alt="Horizontal bar chart of official development assistance (ODA) as a share of gross national income for selected developed countries in 2025, where it shows four countries (Norway, Luxembourg, Sweden, and Denmark) meeting the UN target of 0.7 percent of national income, while most others fall below that level. Source: OECD (2026). License: CC BY." loading="lazy" width="1620" height="1620"/></picture></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-right"><p class="data-insight-dateline latest-data-insights__card-dateline"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="calendar-day" class="svg-inline--fa fa-calendar-day data-insight-dateline__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M128 0c17.7 0 32 14.3 32 32l0 32 128 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32s32 14.3 32 32l0 32 32 0c35.3 0 64 28.7 64 64l0 288c0 35.3-28.7 64-64 64L64 480c-35.3 0-64-28.7-64-64L0 128C0 92.7 28.7 64 64 64l32 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32zm0 256c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32l0 64c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32l64 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32l0-64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32l-64 0z"></path></svg>August 20</p><h3 id="latest-data-insights__card-title-5" class="latest-data-insights__card-title">Four developed countries met the UN’s target for foreign aid in 2025</h3><p class="latest-data-insights__card-authors">Hannah Ritchie and Pablo Arriagada</p><div class="latest-data-insights__card-body"><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">In the 1970s, the UN General Assembly adopted a resolution asking developed countries to contribute at least 0.7% of their national income to foreign aid. Most countries </span><span><span class="">signed off on this goal</span></span><span class="">, except for Switzerland and the United States.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">But very few countries have met this target in the fifty years since then. Even today, only a handful of countries do.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">In 2023, five countries </span><span><span class="">met this target</span></span><span class="">: Norway, Luxembourg, Sweden, Germany, and Denmark. Two years on, this has dropped to four, as Germany’s contribution shrank to 0.6%.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">As you can see in the chart, other developed countries give less than 0.7% of their national income.</span></p><div class="article-block__cta cta">Explore more of our charts on foreign aid: who contributes, and where it goes<svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-continue"><span class="body-3-medium-underlined">Continue reading</span> <svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:10px"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></a></li><li class="latest-data-insights__card"><a class="latest-data-insights__card__data-insight" href="/data-insights/guyanas-oil-driven-economy-has-had-the-largest-gdp-per-capita-growth-in-the-world-in-recent-years" aria-labelledby="latest-data-insights__card-title-6"><div class="image latest-data-insights__card-left"><picture><source srcSet="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/656bec92-4a07-43f8-befc-6521aae9b600/w=48 48w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/656bec92-4a07-43f8-befc-6521aae9b600/w=100 100w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/656bec92-4a07-43f8-befc-6521aae9b600/w=350 350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/656bec92-4a07-43f8-befc-6521aae9b600/w=850 850w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/656bec92-4a07-43f8-befc-6521aae9b600/w=1350 1350w, https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/656bec92-4a07-43f8-befc-6521aae9b600/w=1620 1620w" sizes="(max-width: 960px) 95vw, (min-width: 960px) 533px"/><img src="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/656bec92-4a07-43f8-befc-6521aae9b600/w=1620" alt="Line chart of GDP per capita for selected countries from 1990 to 2026 (international-$ at 2021 prices, adjusted for inflation and differences in living costs) where Guyana&#x27;s line shows a sharp increase after oil production began in December 2019, indicating the fastest recent GDP per capita growth among the countries shown. Data source: Eurostat, OECD, IMF, and World Bank (2026). License: CC BY." loading="lazy" width="1620" height="1620"/></picture></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-right"><p class="data-insight-dateline latest-data-insights__card-dateline"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="calendar-day" class="svg-inline--fa fa-calendar-day data-insight-dateline__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M128 0c17.7 0 32 14.3 32 32l0 32 128 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32s32 14.3 32 32l0 32 32 0c35.3 0 64 28.7 64 64l0 288c0 35.3-28.7 64-64 64L64 480c-35.3 0-64-28.7-64-64L0 128C0 92.7 28.7 64 64 64l32 0 0-32c0-17.7 14.3-32 32-32zm0 256c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32l0 64c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32l64 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32l0-64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32l-64 0z"></path></svg>August 18</p><h3 id="latest-data-insights__card-title-6" class="latest-data-insights__card-title">Guyana’s oil-driven economy has seen the world’s fastest growth in GDP per capita in recent years</h3><p class="latest-data-insights__card-authors">Esteban Ortiz-Ospina and Pablo Arriagada</p><div class="latest-data-insights__card-body"><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">Guyana, a small country in South America, has seen the fastest growth in gross domestic product (</span><span class="dod-span" data-id="gdp" tabindex="0"><span class="">GDP</span></span><span class="">) per capita in the world over the past decade.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">This is illustrated in the chart, which shows GDP per capita for Guyana and several other countries, based on estimates from the World Bank.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">The data is adjusted for inflation, so Guyana’s sharp growth is not due to price changes over time.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">A large and sudden expansion in oil production has driven most of this growth. Between 2020 and 2025, the country’s </span><span><span class="">oil production grew 860%</span></span><span class="">, making it a </span><span><span class="">key contributor to global crude oil supply growth</span></span><span class="">.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">Before oil extraction started, Guyana’s GDP per capita was well below the global average. It is now more than three times higher.</span></p><p class="article-block__text"><span class="">It’s too early to see the full effects of this, and hard to measure how far the oil boom has translated into better living standards in the country. Official poverty estimates, for example, have not been published since production began. But there is early evidence of the government channeling oil revenue toward citizens, for instance, through </span><span><span class="">cash grants for every adult</span></span><span class="">, </span><span><span class="">free tuition at public universities</span></span><span class="">, and </span><span><span class="">increasing health spending</span></span><span class="">.</span></p><div class="article-block__cta cta">Read more about oil production and fossil fuels<svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div><div class="latest-data-insights__card-continue"><span class="body-3-medium-underlined">Continue reading</span> <svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:10px"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></a></li><li class="latest-data-insights__card latest-data-insights__card--see-all"><a href="/latest?type=data-insight" class="owid-btn owid-btn--outline-vermillion latest-data-insights__card__see-all body-3-medium"><span>See all our Data Insights</span><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right owid-btn--icon-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></a></li></ul><button type="button" class="owid-btn owid-btn--solid-blue latest-data-insights__control-button latest-data-insights__control-button--next js--hide-if-js-disabled owid-btn--icon-only" aria-label="Scroll to the next data insight card"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="chevron-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-chevron-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 320 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M311.1 233.4c12.5 12.5 12.5 32.8 0 45.3l-192 192c-12.5 12.5-32.8 12.5-45.3 0s-12.5-32.8 0-45.3L243.2 256 73.9 86.6c-12.5-12.5-12.5-32.8 0-45.3s32.8-12.5 45.3 0l192 192z"></path></svg></button><div class="latest-data-insights__dots js--hide-if-js-disabled"><div class="latest-data-insights__dot latest-data-insights__dot--selected"></div><div class="latest-data-insights__dot"></div><div class="latest-data-insights__dot"></div><div class="latest-data-insights__dot"></div><div class="latest-data-insights__dot"></div><div class="latest-data-insights__dot"></div><div class="latest-data-insights__dot"></div><div class="latest-data-insights__dot latest-data-insights__dot--see-all"></div></div></div></section><section class="key-indicator-collection article-block__key-indicator-collection grid col-start-2 span-cols-12"><header class="key-indicator-collection__header span-cols-8 span-sm-cols-12"><h2 class="h2-bold">Explore our data</h2><p class="body-2-regular">Featured data from our collection of<!-- --> <!-- -->14,090<!-- --> interactive charts.</p></header><a href="/search" class="owid-btn owid-btn--outline-vermillion key-indicator-collection__all-charts-button body-3-medium span-cols-4 col-start-9 col-sm-start-1 span-sm-cols-12"><span>See all our data</span><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right owid-btn--icon-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></a><div class="span-cols-12" data-rac=""><div class="accordion-item accordion-item--open" data-rac="" data-expanded="true"><h3 class="accordion-item__heading"><button id="react-aria-_R_1tcqH1_" class="accordion-item__button" data-rac="" type="button" tabindex="0" data-react-aria-pressable="true" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="react-aria-_R_1tcqH2_" slot="trigger"><div class="key-indicator-header"><div class="key-indicator-header__left"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="chart-line" class="svg-inline--fa fa-chart-line key-indicator-header__tab-icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M64 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32S0 46.3 0 64L0 400c0 44.2 35.8 80 80 80l400 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32L80 416c-8.8 0-16-7.2-16-16L64 64zm406.6 86.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3s-32.8-12.5-45.3 0L320 210.7 262.6 153.4c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0l-96 96c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l73.4-73.4 57.4 57.4c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128z"></path></svg><div><span class="key-indicator-header__title">Child mortality rate</span> <span class="key-indicator-header__source">Long-run estimates combining data from UN &amp; Gapminder</span></div></div></div></button></h3><div class="accordion-item__content" data-rac="" id="react-aria-_R_1tcqH2_" role="region" aria-labelledby="react-aria-_R_1tcqH1_" aria-hidden="false"><div class="key-indicator grid grid-cols-12"><div class="left col-start-1 span-cols-4 span-md-cols-12"><div class="indicator-metadata"><span class="indicator-title">Child mortality rate</span> <span class="indicator-source">Long-run estimates combining data from un &amp; gapminder</span></div><h4 class="narrative-title">What share of children die before their fifth birthday?</h4><div class="text"><p class=""><span class="">What could be more tragic than the death of a young child? Child mortality, the death of children under the age of five, is still </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/much-better-awful-can-be-better" class="span-link"><span class="">extremely common</span></a><span class=""> in our world today.</span></p><p class=""><span class="">The historical data makes clear that it doesn’t have to be this way: it </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/child-mortality-big-problem-in-brief" class="span-link"><span class="">is possible</span></a><span class=""> for societies to protect their children and reduce child mortality to very low rates. For child mortality to reach low levels, many things have to go right at the same time: </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/financing-healthcare" class="span-link"><span class="">good healthcare</span></a><span class="">, </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/hunger-and-undernourishment" class="span-link"><span class="">good nutrition</span></a><span class="">, </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/clean-water-sanitation" class="span-link"><span class="">clean water and sanitation</span></a><span class="">, </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/maternal-mortality" class="span-link"><span class="">maternal health</span></a><span class="">, and high </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/economic-growth" class="span-link"><span class="">living standards</span></a><span class="">. We can, therefore, think of </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/child-mortality" class="span-link"><span class="">child mortality</span></a><span class=""> as a proxy indicator of a country’s living conditions.</span></p><p class=""><span class="">The chart shows our long-run data on child mortality, which allows you to see how child mortality has changed in countries around the world.</span></p></div><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/child-mortality?time=earliest..latest" class="owid-btn owid-btn--solid-blue datapage-link datapage-link-desktop"><span>Explore the data</span><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right owid-btn--icon-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></a></div><div class="key-indicator-chart col-start-5 span-cols-8 span-md-cols-12 margin-0"><div class="owid-chart-frame"><div class="GrapherWithFallback full-width-on-mobile"><figure class="chart GrapherWithFallback__fallback" aria-hidden="false"><picture class="js--hide-if-js-enabled"><source id="grapher-preview-source" srcSet="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/child-mortality.png?time=earliest..latest&amp;imWidth=850 850w, https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/child-mortality.png?time=earliest..latest&amp;imWidth=1700 1700w" sizes="(max-width: 850px) 100vw, 850px"/><img class="GrapherImage" src="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/child-mortality.png?time=earliest..latest" width="850" height="600" loading="lazy"/></picture></figure></div></div></div><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/child-mortality?time=earliest..latest" class="owid-btn owid-btn--solid-blue datapage-link datapage-link-mobile col-start-1 span-cols-12"><span>Explore the data</span><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right owid-btn--icon-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></a></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item accordion-item--closed" data-rac=""><h3 class="accordion-item__heading"><button id="react-aria-_R_2tcqH1_" class="accordion-item__button" data-rac="" type="button" tabindex="0" data-react-aria-pressable="true" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="react-aria-_R_2tcqH2_" slot="trigger"><div class="key-indicator-header"><div class="key-indicator-header__left"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="earth-americas" class="svg-inline--fa fa-earth-americas key-indicator-header__tab-icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M55.7 199.7l30.9 30.9c6 6 14.1 9.4 22.6 9.4l21.5 0c8.5 0 16.6 3.4 22.6 9.4l29.3 29.3c6 6 9.4 14.1 9.4 22.6l0 37.5c0 8.5 3.4 16.6 9.4 22.6l13.3 13.3c6 6 9.4 14.1 9.4 22.6l0 18.7c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32s32-14.3 32-32l0-2.7c0-8.5 3.4-16.6 9.4-22.6l45.3-45.3c6-6 9.4-14.1 9.4-22.6l0-34.7c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32l-82.7 0c-8.5 0-16.6-3.4-22.6-9.4l-16-16c-4.2-4.2-6.6-10-6.6-16 0-12.5 10.1-22.6 22.6-22.6l34.7 0c12.5 0 22.6-10.1 22.6-22.6 0-6-2.4-11.8-6.6-16l-19.7-19.7C242 130 240 125.1 240 120s2-10 5.7-13.7l17.3-17.3c5.8-5.8 9.1-13.7 9.1-21.9 0-7.2-2.4-13.7-6.4-18.9-3.2-.1-6.4-.2-9.6-.2-95.4 0-175.7 64.2-200.3 151.7zM464 256c0-34.6-8.4-67.2-23.4-95.8-6.4 .9-12.7 3.9-17.9 9.1l-13.4 13.4c-6 6-9.4 14.1-9.4 22.6l0 34.7c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32l24.1 0c2.5 0 5-.3 7.3-.8 .4-5 .5-10.1 .5-15.2zM0 256a256 256 0 1 1 512 0 256 256 0 1 1 -512 0z"></path></svg><div><span class="key-indicator-header__title">Share of population living in extreme poverty</span> <span class="key-indicator-header__source">World Bank</span></div></div><div><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="plus" class="svg-inline--fa fa-plus key-indicator-header__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M256 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32s-32 14.3-32 32l0 160-160 0c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l160 0 0 160c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32s32-14.3 32-32l0-160 160 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32l-160 0 0-160z"></path></svg><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right key-indicator-header__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></button></h3><a class="accordion-item__link-mobile" href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/share-of-population-in-extreme-poverty"><div class="key-indicator-header"><div class="key-indicator-header__left"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="earth-americas" class="svg-inline--fa fa-earth-americas key-indicator-header__tab-icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M55.7 199.7l30.9 30.9c6 6 14.1 9.4 22.6 9.4l21.5 0c8.5 0 16.6 3.4 22.6 9.4l29.3 29.3c6 6 9.4 14.1 9.4 22.6l0 37.5c0 8.5 3.4 16.6 9.4 22.6l13.3 13.3c6 6 9.4 14.1 9.4 22.6l0 18.7c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32s32-14.3 32-32l0-2.7c0-8.5 3.4-16.6 9.4-22.6l45.3-45.3c6-6 9.4-14.1 9.4-22.6l0-34.7c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32l-82.7 0c-8.5 0-16.6-3.4-22.6-9.4l-16-16c-4.2-4.2-6.6-10-6.6-16 0-12.5 10.1-22.6 22.6-22.6l34.7 0c12.5 0 22.6-10.1 22.6-22.6 0-6-2.4-11.8-6.6-16l-19.7-19.7C242 130 240 125.1 240 120s2-10 5.7-13.7l17.3-17.3c5.8-5.8 9.1-13.7 9.1-21.9 0-7.2-2.4-13.7-6.4-18.9-3.2-.1-6.4-.2-9.6-.2-95.4 0-175.7 64.2-200.3 151.7zM464 256c0-34.6-8.4-67.2-23.4-95.8-6.4 .9-12.7 3.9-17.9 9.1l-13.4 13.4c-6 6-9.4 14.1-9.4 22.6l0 34.7c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32l24.1 0c2.5 0 5-.3 7.3-.8 .4-5 .5-10.1 .5-15.2zM0 256a256 256 0 1 1 512 0 256 256 0 1 1 -512 0z"></path></svg><div><span class="key-indicator-header__title">Share of population living in extreme poverty</span> <span class="key-indicator-header__source">World Bank</span></div></div><div><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="plus" class="svg-inline--fa fa-plus key-indicator-header__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M256 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32s-32 14.3-32 32l0 160-160 0c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l160 0 0 160c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32s32-14.3 32-32l0-160 160 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32l-160 0 0-160z"></path></svg><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right key-indicator-header__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></a><div class="accordion-item__content" data-rac="" id="react-aria-_R_2tcqH2_" role="region" aria-labelledby="react-aria-_R_2tcqH1_" aria-hidden="true" hidden=""><div class="key-indicator grid grid-cols-12"><div class="left col-start-1 span-cols-4 span-md-cols-12"><div class="indicator-metadata"><span class="indicator-title">Share of population living in extreme poverty</span> <span class="indicator-source">World bank</span></div><h4 class="narrative-title">What share of the population lives in extreme poverty?</h4><div class="text"><p class=""><span class="">The UN sets the “International Poverty Line” as a worldwide comparable definition for extreme poverty. Extreme poverty is currently defined as living on less than $3 per day. This indicator, published by the World Bank, has successfully drawn attention to the terrible depths of poverty of the poorest people in the world.</span></p><p class=""><span class="">Two centuries ago, </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/extreme-poverty-in-brief" class="span-link"><span class="">the majority</span></a><span class=""> of the world’s population was extremely poor. Back then, it was widely believed that widespread poverty was inevitable. This turned out to be wrong. Economic growth is possible and </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/what-is-economic-growth" class="span-link"><span class="">makes it possible</span></a><span class=""> for entire societies to leave the deep poverty of the past behind. Whether or not countries are leaving the worst poverty behind can be monitored by relying on this indicator.</span></p></div><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/share-of-population-in-extreme-poverty" class="owid-btn owid-btn--solid-blue datapage-link datapage-link-desktop"><span>Explore the data</span><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right owid-btn--icon-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></a></div><div class="key-indicator-chart col-start-5 span-cols-8 span-md-cols-12 margin-0"><div class="owid-chart-frame"><div class="GrapherWithFallback full-width-on-mobile"><figure class="chart GrapherWithFallback__fallback" aria-hidden="false"><picture class="js--hide-if-js-enabled"><source id="grapher-preview-source" srcSet="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/share-of-population-in-extreme-poverty.png?imWidth=850 850w, https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/share-of-population-in-extreme-poverty.png?imWidth=1700 1700w" sizes="(max-width: 850px) 100vw, 850px"/><img class="GrapherImage" src="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/share-of-population-in-extreme-poverty.png" width="850" height="600" loading="lazy"/></picture></figure></div></div></div><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/share-of-population-in-extreme-poverty" class="owid-btn owid-btn--solid-blue datapage-link datapage-link-mobile col-start-1 span-cols-12"><span>Explore the data</span><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right owid-btn--icon-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></a></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item accordion-item--closed" data-rac=""><h3 class="accordion-item__heading"><button id="react-aria-_R_3tcqH1_" class="accordion-item__button" data-rac="" type="button" tabindex="0" data-react-aria-pressable="true" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="react-aria-_R_3tcqH2_" slot="trigger"><div class="key-indicator-header"><div class="key-indicator-header__left"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="chart-line" class="svg-inline--fa fa-chart-line key-indicator-header__tab-icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M64 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32S0 46.3 0 64L0 400c0 44.2 35.8 80 80 80l400 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32L80 416c-8.8 0-16-7.2-16-16L64 64zm406.6 86.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3s-32.8-12.5-45.3 0L320 210.7 262.6 153.4c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0l-96 96c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l73.4-73.4 57.4 57.4c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128z"></path></svg><div><span class="key-indicator-header__title">Life expectancy</span> <span class="key-indicator-header__source">Long-run estimates collated from multiple sources by Our World in Data</span></div></div><div><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="plus" class="svg-inline--fa fa-plus key-indicator-header__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M256 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32s-32 14.3-32 32l0 160-160 0c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l160 0 0 160c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32s32-14.3 32-32l0-160 160 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32l-160 0 0-160z"></path></svg><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right key-indicator-header__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></button></h3><a class="accordion-item__link-mobile" href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/life-expectancy"><div class="key-indicator-header"><div class="key-indicator-header__left"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="chart-line" class="svg-inline--fa fa-chart-line key-indicator-header__tab-icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M64 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32S0 46.3 0 64L0 400c0 44.2 35.8 80 80 80l400 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32L80 416c-8.8 0-16-7.2-16-16L64 64zm406.6 86.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3s-32.8-12.5-45.3 0L320 210.7 262.6 153.4c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0l-96 96c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l73.4-73.4 57.4 57.4c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128z"></path></svg><div><span class="key-indicator-header__title">Life expectancy</span> <span class="key-indicator-header__source">Long-run estimates collated from multiple sources by Our World in Data</span></div></div><div><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="plus" class="svg-inline--fa fa-plus key-indicator-header__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M256 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32s-32 14.3-32 32l0 160-160 0c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l160 0 0 160c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32s32-14.3 32-32l0-160 160 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32l-160 0 0-160z"></path></svg><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right key-indicator-header__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></a><div class="accordion-item__content" data-rac="" id="react-aria-_R_3tcqH2_" role="region" aria-labelledby="react-aria-_R_3tcqH1_" aria-hidden="true" hidden=""><div class="key-indicator grid grid-cols-12"><div class="left col-start-1 span-cols-4 span-md-cols-12"><div class="indicator-metadata"><span class="indicator-title">Life expectancy</span> <span class="indicator-source">Long-run estimates collated from multiple sources by our world in data</span></div><h4 class="narrative-title">How has people’s life expectancy changed over time?</h4><div class="text"><p class=""><span class="">Across the world, people are living longer. In 1900, the global average life expectancy of a newborn was 32 years. By 2021, this had more than doubled to 71 years.</span></p><p class=""><span class="">Big improvements were achieved by countries </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/life-expectancy-globally" class="span-link"><span class="">around the world</span></a><span class="">. The chart shows that life expectancy has more than doubled in every region of the world. This improvement is not only due to declining child mortality; life expectancy increased </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/its-not-just-about-child-mortality-life-expectancy-improved-at-all-ages" class="span-link"><span class="">at all ages</span></a><span class="">.</span></p><p class=""><span class="">This visualization shows long-run estimates of life expectancy brought together by our team from several different data sources. It also shows that the </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/coronavirus" class="span-link"><span class="">COVID-19 pandemic</span></a><span class=""> led to reduced life expectancy worldwide.</span></p></div><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/life-expectancy" class="owid-btn owid-btn--solid-blue datapage-link datapage-link-desktop"><span>Explore the data</span><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right owid-btn--icon-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></a></div><div class="key-indicator-chart col-start-5 span-cols-8 span-md-cols-12 margin-0"><div class="owid-chart-frame"><div class="GrapherWithFallback full-width-on-mobile"><figure class="chart GrapherWithFallback__fallback" aria-hidden="false"><picture class="js--hide-if-js-enabled"><source id="grapher-preview-source" srcSet="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/life-expectancy.png?imWidth=850 850w, https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/life-expectancy.png?imWidth=1700 1700w" sizes="(max-width: 850px) 100vw, 850px"/><img class="GrapherImage" src="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/life-expectancy.png" width="850" height="600" loading="lazy"/></picture></figure></div></div></div><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/life-expectancy" class="owid-btn owid-btn--solid-blue datapage-link datapage-link-mobile col-start-1 span-cols-12"><span>Explore the data</span><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right owid-btn--icon-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></a></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item accordion-item--closed" data-rac=""><h3 class="accordion-item__heading"><button id="react-aria-_R_4tcqH1_" class="accordion-item__button" data-rac="" type="button" tabindex="0" data-react-aria-pressable="true" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="react-aria-_R_4tcqH2_" slot="trigger"><div class="key-indicator-header"><div class="key-indicator-header__left"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="chart-line" class="svg-inline--fa fa-chart-line key-indicator-header__tab-icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M64 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32S0 46.3 0 64L0 400c0 44.2 35.8 80 80 80l400 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32L80 416c-8.8 0-16-7.2-16-16L64 64zm406.6 86.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3s-32.8-12.5-45.3 0L320 210.7 262.6 153.4c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0l-96 96c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l73.4-73.4 57.4 57.4c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128z"></path></svg><div><span class="key-indicator-header__title">CO₂ emissions per capita</span> <span class="key-indicator-header__source">Long-run estimates from the Global Carbon Budget</span></div></div><div><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="plus" class="svg-inline--fa fa-plus key-indicator-header__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M256 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32s-32 14.3-32 32l0 160-160 0c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l160 0 0 160c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32s32-14.3 32-32l0-160 160 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32l-160 0 0-160z"></path></svg><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right key-indicator-header__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></button></h3><a class="accordion-item__link-mobile" href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/co-emissions-per-capita?tab=chart&amp;country=USA~GBR~CHN~ZAF~PAN"><div class="key-indicator-header"><div class="key-indicator-header__left"><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="chart-line" class="svg-inline--fa fa-chart-line key-indicator-header__tab-icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M64 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32S0 46.3 0 64L0 400c0 44.2 35.8 80 80 80l400 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32L80 416c-8.8 0-16-7.2-16-16L64 64zm406.6 86.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3s-32.8-12.5-45.3 0L320 210.7 262.6 153.4c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0l-96 96c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l73.4-73.4 57.4 57.4c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128z"></path></svg><div><span class="key-indicator-header__title">CO₂ emissions per capita</span> <span class="key-indicator-header__source">Long-run estimates from the Global Carbon Budget</span></div></div><div><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="plus" class="svg-inline--fa fa-plus key-indicator-header__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 448 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M256 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32s-32 14.3-32 32l0 160-160 0c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l160 0 0 160c0 17.7 14.3 32 32 32s32-14.3 32-32l0-160 160 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32l-160 0 0-160z"></path></svg><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right key-indicator-header__icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></div></div></a><div class="accordion-item__content" data-rac="" id="react-aria-_R_4tcqH2_" role="region" aria-labelledby="react-aria-_R_4tcqH1_" aria-hidden="true" hidden=""><div class="key-indicator grid grid-cols-12"><div class="left col-start-1 span-cols-4 span-md-cols-12"><div class="indicator-metadata"><span class="indicator-title">CO₂ emissions per capita</span> <span class="indicator-source">Long-run estimates from the global carbon budget</span></div><h4 class="narrative-title">How have CO₂ emissions per capita changed?</h4><div class="text"><p class=""><span class="">The main source of carbon dioxide (CO</span><sub><span class="">2</span></sub><span class="">) emissions is the burning of fossil fuels. It is the primary </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/co2-and-greenhouse-gas-emissions" class="span-link"><span class="">greenhouse gas</span></a><span class=""> causing </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/explorers/climate-change" class="span-link span-linked-chart"><span class="">climate change</span><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="chart-line" class="svg-inline--fa fa-chart-line span-linked-chart-icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M64 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32S0 46.3 0 64L0 400c0 44.2 35.8 80 80 80l400 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32L80 416c-8.8 0-16-7.2-16-16L64 64zm406.6 86.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3s-32.8-12.5-45.3 0L320 210.7 262.6 153.4c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0l-96 96c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l73.4-73.4 57.4 57.4c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128z"></path></svg></a><span class="">.</span></p><p class=""><span class="">Globally, CO</span><sub><span class="">2</span></sub><span class=""> emissions have remained at just below 5 tonnes per person for over a decade. Between countries, however, there are large differences, and while emissions are rapidly increasing in some countries, they are rapidly falling in others.</span></p><p class=""><span class="">The source for this CO</span><sub><span class="">2</span></sub><span class=""> data is the Global Carbon Budget, a dataset we update yearly as soon as it is published. In addition to these production-based emissions, they publish consumption-based emissions for the last three decades, which can be viewed in our </span><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/explorers/co2?facet=none&amp;country=CHN~USA~IND~FRA&amp;Gas+or+Warming=CO%E2%82%82&amp;Accounting=Consumption-based&amp;Fuel+or+Land+Use+Change=All+fossil+emissions&amp;Count=Per+capita" class="span-link span-linked-chart"><span class="">Greenhouse Gas Emissions Data Explorer</span><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="chart-line" class="svg-inline--fa fa-chart-line span-linked-chart-icon" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M64 64c0-17.7-14.3-32-32-32S0 46.3 0 64L0 400c0 44.2 35.8 80 80 80l400 0c17.7 0 32-14.3 32-32s-14.3-32-32-32L80 416c-8.8 0-16-7.2-16-16L64 64zm406.6 86.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3s-32.8-12.5-45.3 0L320 210.7 262.6 153.4c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0l-96 96c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l73.4-73.4 57.4 57.4c12.5 12.5 32.8 12.5 45.3 0l128-128z"></path></svg></a><span class="">.</span></p></div><a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/co-emissions-per-capita?tab=chart&amp;country=USA~GBR~CHN~ZAF~PAN" class="owid-btn owid-btn--solid-blue datapage-link datapage-link-desktop"><span>Explore the data</span><svg data-prefix="fas" data-icon="arrow-right" class="svg-inline--fa fa-arrow-right owid-btn--icon-right" role="img" viewBox="0 0 512 512" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M502.6 278.6c12.5-12.5 12.5-32.8 0-45.3l-160-160c-12.5-12.5-32.8-12.5-45.3 0s-12.5 32.8 0 45.3L402.7 224 32 224c-17.7 0-32 14.3-32 32s14.3 32 32 32l370.7 0-105.4 105.4c-12.5 12.5-12.5 32.8 0 45.3s32.8 12.5 45.3 0l160-160z"></path></svg></a></div><div class="key-indicator-chart col-start-5 span-cols-8 span-md-cols-12 margin-0"><div class="owid-chart-frame"><div class="GrapherWithFallback full-width-on-mobile"><figure class="chart GrapherWithFallback__fallback" aria-hidden="false"><picture class="js--hide-if-js-enabled"><source id="grapher-preview-source" srcSet="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/co-emissions-per-capita.png?tab=chart&amp;country=USA~GBR~CHN~ZAF~PAN&amp;imWidth=850 850w, https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/co-emissions-per-capita.png?tab=chart&amp;country=USA~GBR~CHN~ZAF~PAN&amp;imWidth=1700 1700w" sizes="(max-width: 850px) 100vw, 850px"/><img class="Gr